WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.941
You replace in increments, with
what's called the strangler fig.

2
00:00:03.941 --> 00:00:07.533
A façade sits in front of the
legacy application and routes

3
00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:10.975
each request either to the old
system or to a new service.

4
00:00:10.975 --> 00:00:13.972
Functions move one at a time
behind the same contract,

5
00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:17.650
until nothing depends on the old
system and it can be switched off.

6
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.185
Rollback stays possible until
the old tables are removed,

7
00:00:21.185 --> 00:00:23.100
so that's a deliberate last step.

8
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.225
It doesn't fit everything; the
requests have to be interceptable.

9
00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:31.170
So at design review, ask every
refactor to name its façade,

10
00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:35.887
its first function, its plan for
shared data and when rollback ends.

11
00:00:35.887 --> 00:00:37.300
Then, the data.
